think the producers that  violence is such a part of  growing up it's uh  the moment i think everybody realizes  they lose a certain amount of innocence  when they realize they could be  threatened or people they care about  could be threatened or  they could be the bully they could also  be the one  with the gavin's capacity for violence  and  i  people have asked  me not just on this film but other films  why i'm interested in the topic and i  think  and and sometimes they say because  you're a woman why are you interested in  violence what draws people to violence  particularly in schools what draws young  people to create families outside of  probably the family at home that's not  so good they find something protective  here  and it's that dual nature of protection  but what we propagate is violence  is fascinating to  me as somebody who's i don't have  children but  maternal  what is the world that we are  raising kids in what are we teaching  people how do we use moments of violence  to  to to have an educational moment and i  think that that's probably one of the  things that women love about this book  this one more than any  story i know um  had to find a way to  deliver on the  genre of spectacle science fiction  action  but it's essentially a  very much a character story with some  incredibly complex themes  so i think gavin  figured it out somehow well i had a lot  of debate with my producers over it i  mean i i will just say to that that part  of what we're trying to do here  is is perhaps a little bit manipulative  it's a sleight of hand  um this is a story about kids  embracing games that can be violent and  i'm fascinated by the way games and  reality are starting to merge  in terms of the way video games are more  and more photo real  but reality is more and more game like  we watch wars on tv  we see it on cnn  we fight with drones we can be detached  from the battlefield and i think lynn  raises an incredibly important point is  how do you give t