nk another  really  interesting challenge that we had is  we say there's really no such thing as  post-production anymore and and this  kind of film really defies the kind of  conventional  uh you know  development prep shoot post kind of  model  and and for gavin you know  in so many ways the directing doesn't  stop on the last shoot day right it  there really is  physical production and digital  production  and and and both are  um in in inextricably linked and and in  many cases you know the entire scene is  generated in a computer or there are  actors against a green screen and the  world around them is being generated and  it's so ambitious and there's so many  layers and there's so much going on that  it's it's like three-dimensional chess i  think i i think for gavin he's got the  hardest job of anybody  on any film uh as the writer and the  director of that story world and another  thing is like when we read the  description we read gavin's story in his  adaptation  the words on the page all kind of  conjure up different images in our  mind's eye and as we all sit around and  talk we realize  we're all going in different places and  so i think another wonderful thing we're  able to do on this picture is is during  the development process literally put  the power of these digital tools in  gavin's hands to realize the visual  language of the movie  before we go into production so in many  ways he's the architect of the of the  story's world and and now we're all on  the same page and we know what that  battle room looks like in zero gravity  we have a blueprint of gavin's design we  have no question what's going to happen  when we go in there so as we move into  physical production  we're not trying to figure that out in  that moment it's really gavin focusing  on the performance with these kids in  the book the the battle room takes place  in a black those who've read the book it  takes place in a black box  and when you read the book you don't  mind and the